The Civilian Secretariat for the Police Service has published the draft Second-Hand Goods Amendment Bill 2026, and is asking you to comment.
The draft Bill seeks to amend the Second-Hand Goods Act, 2009 (Act No. 6 of 2009), in order to-
• provide for proof of registration regarding value added tax in respect of registration as a dealer;
• provide for a valid business visa for a foreigner to be able to be registered as a dealer;
• compel a dealer to retain personal information subject to the provisions of the Protection of Personal Information Act, 2013;
• provide for the position of dealers who deal from different premises and where goods are moved between premises. A provision is inserted to clarify the position of auctioneers who deal in second-hand goods;
• provide for persons under the age 18 years to pawn goods, subject to authorization;
• provide for the recording of microdot particulars that are applied in vehicles;
• ensure that jewelers do not acquire unwrought metal directly from unlicensed producers;
• provide for the proper recording of particulars of second-hand communication equipment;
• provide for routine inspections by knowledgeable persons;
• provide that goods may be seized in terms of search and seizure warrants to be issued under the Criminal Procedure Act, 1977 (Act No. 51 of 1977;
• provide for offences, penalties as well as administration sanctions;
• provide for the exemption of non-profit organisations registered under the Nonprofit Organisations Act, 1997 from the provisions of the SecondHand Goods Act, 2009;
• provide for the amendment of Schedules 1, 2, and 3 in order to align penalties with statutory offences.;
• provide for the insertion of Schedule 5;
• provide for the amendment of Schedule 5 of the Criminal Procedure Act, 1977;
• provide for the amendment of Schedule 2 of the Criminal Law Amendment Act, 1997, and to provide for matters connected therewith
